.TH SP2P 1 "September 20, 2026" "sp2p" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sp2p \- secure peer-to-peer file transfer, rsync, and tunnels
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sp2p send
.RI [ flags ]
.IR file | folder | ... | \-
.br
.B sp2p receive
.RI [ flags ]
.I CODE
.br
.B sp2p rsync send
.RI [ flags ]
.B \-\-
.I RSYNC_ARGS
.br
.B sp2p rsync send
.RI [ flags ]
.I DIRECTORY
.br
.B sp2p rsync recv
.RI [ flags ]
.I CODE DIRECTORY
.br
.B sp2p rsync recv
.RI [ flags ]
.I CODE
.B \-\-
.I RSYNC_ARGS
.br
.B sp2p tunnel serve
.RI [ flags ]
.BR \-\-to " ENDPOINT | " \-\-stdio
.br
.B sp2p tunnel connect
.RI [ flags ]
.BR \-\-listen " ENDPOINT " CODE " | " \-\-stdio " CODE"
.br
.B sp2p version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sp2p
moves data directly between two computers over TCP or WebRTC.
Everything is end-to-end encrypted; the signaling server only helps the two
peers find each other and never sees the contents.
.PP
One side creates a session and gets a short code.
The other side enters that code to connect.
The code is a secret: anyone who has it can join the session.
If no direct connection can be made, an encrypted TURN relay is offered
(after confirmation, unless
.B \-allow\-relay
is given).
.PP
There are three kinds of session:
.IP \(bu 2
.B File transfer
sends files, folders, or stdin in one direction, verified on arrival.
The receiver can be the CLI or a browser.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Rsync
carries an ordinary rsync run between two machines. See
.BR RSYNC .
.IP \(bu 2
.B Tunnels
forward one TCP port, Unix socket, or stdin/stdout pair. See
.BR TUNNELS .
.PP
For scripts and AI agents, add
.B \-format json
to any transfer command to get newline-delimited JSON events. See
.BR "JSON EVENTS" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B send
Send one or more files or folders.
A transfer code is displayed for the receiver.
Use
.B \-
to read from stdin.
.TP
.B receive
Receive a transfer using the sender's code.
.B recv
is accepted as an alias.
.TP
.B rsync send
Create a code and act as the rsync source.
Either run the rsync client with exact arguments after
.BR \-\- ,
or expose one directory read-only so the receiver can choose the options.
.TP
.B rsync recv
Join the sender's code.
Either expose a write-only destination directory, or run the rsync client with
exact arguments after
.BR \-\- .
.TP
.B tunnel serve
Create a code and expose one fixed TCP or Unix target, or stdin/stdout.
.TP
.B tunnel connect
Join a tunnel code and open one local TCP or Unix listener, or stdin/stdout.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH SEND FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-compress " level"
zstd compression level, 0 to disable, 1\(en9 for increasing compression.
Default: 3.
.TP
.BI \-name " filename"
Filename to use when reading from stdin.
.TP
.B \-allow\-relay
Allow TURN relay connections without prompting.
.TP
.BI \-server " url"
Signaling server URL.
Give an https:// URL and the /ws path is added for you, or a full WebSocket
endpoint such as wss://host/ws.
Default: value of
.B SP2P_SERVER
or
.IR https://sp2p.io .
.TP
.BI \-url " url"
Public base URL for share links.
Default: value of
.B SP2P_URL
or the server URL.
.TP
.BI \-transport " mode"
Transport to use for the P2P connection.
Values:
.BR auto ", " tcp ", " webrtc .
Default:
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI \-parallel " count"
Parallel TCP connections: 0 for automatic, 1 for a single connection, or 2\(en6
to force a count.
Default: 0.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose diagnostic output.
.TP
.BI \-format " format"
Output format:
.B human
or newline-delimited JSON events with
.BR json .
Default:
.BR human .
.TP
.BI \-event\-output " stream"
Where JSON events are written:
.B stdout
or
.BR stderr .
Default:
.BR stdout .
.TP
.BI \-status\-file " path"
Atomically write the latest private JSON status snapshot to
.IR path .
Requires
.BR \-format " json."
The snapshot includes the transfer code after session registration.
.SH RECEIVE FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-output " dir"
Output directory for received files.
Default:
.IR . " (current directory)."
.TP
.B \-stdout
Write received data to stdout instead of saving to a file.
.TP
.BI \-max\-receive\-bytes " bytes"
Maximum decoded transfer bytes, including unknown-size stdin.
Default: 0, meaning 1 TiB (1099511627776 bytes), not unlimited.
Raise it only for transfers you trust.
.TP
.BI \-max\-extract\-bytes " bytes"
Maximum expanded archive bytes, including sparse logical output.
Default: 0, meaning 1 TiB. Independent of the receive byte limit.
.TP
.B \-allow\-relay
Allow TURN relay connections without prompting.
.TP
.BI \-server " url"
Signaling server URL.
Give an https:// URL and the /ws path is added for you, or a full WebSocket
endpoint such as wss://host/ws.
Default: value of
.B SP2P_SERVER
or
.IR https://sp2p.io .
.TP
.BI \-transport " mode"
Transport to use for the P2P connection.
Values:
.BR auto ", " tcp ", " webrtc .
Default:
.BR auto .
.TP
.BI \-parallel " count"
Parallel TCP connections: 0 for automatic, 1 for a single connection, or 2\(en6
to force a count.
Default: 0.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose diagnostic output.
.TP
.BI \-format " format"
Output format:
.B human
or newline-delimited JSON events with
.BR json .
Default:
.BR human .
.TP
.BI \-event\-output " stream"
Where JSON events are written:
.B stdout
or
.BR stderr .
Default:
.BR stdout .
When using
.BR \-stdout " to receive file bytes, set this to " stderr
so events do not corrupt the output.
.TP
.BI \-status\-file " path"
Atomically write the latest private JSON status snapshot to
.IR path .
Requires
.BR \-format " json."
.SH RECEIVED OUTPUT
Received content is verified against the sender's byte count and SHA-256,
closed, and published before the sender is told the transfer succeeded.
.PP
Existing files are never replaced.
A colliding file name receives an available numbered name.
An archive is extracted into one new directory and fails if that directory
already exists; it is never merged into existing output.
A single folder keeps its own name; several paths sent together get a wrapper
directory.
Filesystems that cannot publish without replacing fail explicitly.
.PP
Publication is not a power-loss durability guarantee.
Output written to stdout cannot be rolled back.
If the final acknowledgement is lost, the receiver may hold valid output while
the sender reports failure.
Healthy paused stdin and slow output finalization keep peer heartbeats
flowing; physical network writes have a two-minute timeout.
After sending Complete, a receiver waits at most five seconds for the final
shutdown acknowledgement.
.SH RSYNC
.B sp2p rsync
runs the rsync already installed on each machine over an encrypted SP2P
stream, so an incremental sync works between machines that cannot reach each
other directly. No SSH is involved.
.PP
The source always runs
.B rsync send
and creates the code; only
.B rsync recv
takes a code.
Either side can supply the rsync options, and the other side just exposes a
directory.
.PP
Sender-selected options:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p rsync send -- -av --partial ./photos/ sp2p::share/
$ mkdir -p ./backup
$ sp2p rsync recv CODE ./backup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receiver-selected options:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p rsync send ./photos
$ sp2p rsync recv CODE -- -av --partial sp2p::share/ ./photos/
.fi
.RE
.PP
Everything after the required
.B \-\-
boundary is passed to rsync unchanged, preserving order and quoting.
Put SP2P flags such as
.B \-\-server
and
.B \-\-rsync\-binary
before it.
.B sp2p::share/
is a placeholder for the remote side:
.B sp2p
is the host and
.B share
is the fixed module name.
.SS Platform support
Rsync works on macOS and Linux, including Apple's built-in openrsync and the
historical macOS rsync 2.6.9; no Homebrew replacement is required.
On Windows, run both SP2P and rsync inside WSL; the native Windows adapter is
not supported.
Use
.BI \-\-rsync\-binary " path"
to select a specific executable.
Available options, metadata behavior, cross-version interoperability, and rsync
security fixes depend on the rsync implementations installed on both peers.
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu 2
Each command pair performs one rsync invocation.
Running again reuses the existing files and partials for rsync's delta
comparison, but needs a new sender-created code.
.IP \(bu 2
A receiver that exposes a directory is write-only and refuses sender-requested
deletion unless it is given
.B \-\-allow\-delete
before the code.
A receiver running the rsync client controls deletion through its own rsync
arguments.
.IP \(bu 2
Rsync controls file selection, metadata, compression, partial files, deltas,
and link handling.
SP2P's receive limits and no-replace publication do not apply.
.IP \(bu 2
Before serving, SP2P refuses a directory that contains a symlink resolving to
a directory.
File and dangling symlinks can remain links under
.B \-a
with munged targets, and peer-requested
.BR copy-links ,
.BR copy-unsafe-links ,
.BR copy-dirlinks ,
and
.B keep-dirlinks
behavior is refused.
This is defense in depth, not an operating-system sandbox; control who can
modify a served tree.
.IP \(bu 2
Write-only mode blocks downloads but does not hide the existing-file
information that rsync's delta protocol exchanges.
.IP \(bu 2
SP2P configures rsync's transport itself:
.B RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG
for upstream rsync, or a fixed local
.B \-e
helper for openrsync.
User-supplied
.B \-e
and remote-shell options are rejected.
Do not set
.B RSYNC_CONNECT_PROG
or invoke SP2P's hidden helper yourself.
.IP \(bu 2
A connection or a partial file is not completion.
Wait for both commands to exit successfully, or for a JSON
.B result
event with a completed outcome.
.SH TUNNELS
.B sp2p tunnel
forwards one TCP port or Unix socket from one machine to another.
.B tunnel serve
runs on the machine that can reach the service and creates the code.
.B tunnel connect
joins with that code and opens one local listener.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p tunnel serve --to tcp://127.0.0.1:5432
$ sp2p tunnel connect --listen tcp://127.0.0.1:15432 CODE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Wait for the listener's ready message (or the JSON
.B ready
event) before starting a local client.
Unix sockets work the same way, and the two ends may be mixed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p tunnel serve --to unix:///run/example/service.sock
$ sp2p tunnel connect --listen unix:///tmp/sp2p-example.sock CODE

$ sp2p tunnel serve --to unix:///run/example/service.sock
$ sp2p tunnel connect --listen tcp://127.0.0.1:15432 CODE
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-stdio
attaches the tunnel to stdin and stdout instead of a socket, giving a raw
full-duplex byte stream between two processes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p tunnel serve --stdio
$ sp2p tunnel connect --stdio CODE
.fi
.RE
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu 2
Each code carries exactly one connection.
The listener closes after accepting it and does not reconnect or replay;
create a new code to connect again.
.IP \(bu 2
The serving side fixes the target.
The connecting peer cannot choose a different host, port, or path, and SP2P
dials the target only after the peer is authenticated and requests the stream.
.IP \(bu 2
TCP listeners need at least a port; an omitted host binds loopback, which is
recommended anyway.
A
.B \-\-to
Unix path must be an existing service socket, and a
.B \-\-listen
Unix path must not already exist.
SP2P creates that listener privately and removes only the socket it created.
.IP \(bu 2
Directional EOF is preserved where the endpoint supports half-close, so one
side can stop sending and continue receiving.
Cancellation closes the stream and the local endpoints.
.IP \(bu 2
Stdio tunnels differ from
.BR "sp2p send -" :
file-transfer stdin is a finite, verified, one-way file, while tunnel stdio is
full-duplex and unframed.
Tunnel stdout carries payload only, so JSON mode requires
.B \-\-event\-output stderr.
.SH RSYNC AND TUNNEL FLAGS
.TP
.BI \-\-server " url"
Signaling server URL: an https:// URL (the /ws path is added for you) or a
full WebSocket endpoint such as wss://host/ws.
Both peers must use the same server.
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " mode"
Connection method:
.BR auto ", " tcp ", or " webrtc .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-relay
Allow an encrypted TURN relay without prompting.
.TP
.BI \-\-format " format"
Output format:
.B human
or newline-delimited
.BR json .
.TP
.BI \-\-event\-output " stream"
JSON event destination:
.B stdout
or
.BR stderr .
Stdio tunnels require stderr.
.TP
.BI \-\-status\-file " path"
Atomically publish the latest private JSON snapshot. Requires JSON format.
.TP
.B \-\-v
Emit verbose diagnostics.
.TP
.BI \-\-rsync\-binary " path"
Rsync commands only. Select the installed rsync or openrsync executable.
Default:
.BR rsync .
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-delete
Only for
.BR "rsync recv CODE DIRECTORY" .
Authorize sender-requested deletion inside that destination.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " endpoint"
Tunnel serve only. Fixed
.B tcp://HOST:PORT
or
.B unix:///PATH
target.
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " endpoint"
Tunnel connect only. One-use
.B tcp://HOST:PORT
or
.B unix:///PATH
listener.
.TP
.B \-\-stdio
Tunnel only. Bridge the peer stream to stdin/stdout instead of a socket.
.TP
.BI \-\-accept\-timeout " duration"
Tunnel only. Maximum wait for a local listener connection. Default: 5 minutes.
.SH JSON EVENTS
With
.BR \-format " json,"
every command writes one JSON object per line.
Each event includes
.BR schema_version " (1), " sequence ", " at ", " event ", and " role .
Rsync and tunnel events also include
.B service
and
.BR mode :
tunnel role and mode are
.B serve
or
.BR connect ;
rsync roles are
.B send
or
.B recv
with mode
.BR send-client ", " send-daemon ", " recv-client ", or " recv-daemon .
.PP
The side that creates the session
.RB ( send ", " "rsync send" ", or " "tunnel serve" )
emits a
.B session
event containing the secret code.
Only file transfers also include a browser
.BR share_url .
A
.B ready
event reports local readiness; its
.B endpoint
identifies a socket listener or rsync daemon directory and can be absent in
stdio or client modes.
.B progress
events report
.B bytes_transferred
and
.B total_bytes
for files, or cumulative
.B bytes_sent
and
.B bytes_received
for streams.
.PP
An
.B error
event is diagnostic, not terminal.
Keep reading until exactly one
.B result
event arrives with an
.B outcome
of
.B completed
or
.BR failed .
Stream results include final byte counters; a failed subprocess is reported in
the result's
.BR error .
.PP
When a direct connection fails and a relay is available, a
.B relay_required
event names a temporary owner-only response file.
Write
.B allow
or
.B deny
to that file to answer the prompt; SP2P removes it after reading.
.PP
In human rsync client modes, the child's stdin, stdout, and stderr are attached
normally and SP2P preserves its numeric exit status.
In daemon modes, the child's stdin and stdout carry the rsync protocol and its
diagnostics go to stderr.
In JSON mode, client stdout/stderr and daemon stderr are captured as
.B subprocess_output
events, with
.B output_stream
naming the stream and
.B output_data
holding the exact bytes in base64.
.PP
Rsync and tunnel
.B \-\-help
in JSON mode emits one
.B result
with a completed outcome, help mode, and the usage text in its
.BR message ,
without contacting the server.
.PP
The complete event contract with examples is published at
.IR https://sp2p.io/llm .
.SH PROTOCOL COMPATIBILITY
Compatibility is automatic.
Two updated peers use transfer protocol v3; a transfer with a 0.4.0 peer uses
v2.
Both work through either server version, and clients and servers can be
upgraded independently.
No version flag or browser checkbox is needed.
.PP
Capability markers are bound to key derivation and confirmation, so tampering
fails authentication instead of silently downgrading updated peers.
Every v3 peer, including browsers, authenticates connection candidates and
sender selection.
Connection errors never trigger a retry at a lower version.
.PP
A v2 transfer warns without prompting (a
.B warning
event in JSON mode), disables parallel TCP, and lacks v3 candidate
authentication and receive credits.
Its idle and backpressure behavior is weaker, and a slow browser receiver can
exceed its bounded input queue.
Updated peers keep their local decoding, queue, quota, and output protections;
old peers keep their old defects.
Upgrade the older peer for the full protections.
JSON mode emits a
.B protocol
event after key confirmation, and later events and status snapshots report the
authenticated version.
.SH BOOTSTRAP SCRIPTS
The signaling server also serves shell and PowerShell scripts that download a
fixed release of
.BR sp2p ,
run one transfer, and delete it, so that a peer can send or receive without
installing anything.
Shell scripts need curl or wget and one of sha256sum, shasum, or openssl;
PowerShell uses built-in commands.
The archive is checked against the same release's checksum manifest before
extraction, and a missing hash tool or a bad checksum stops the script.
This trusts the GitHub HTTPS release channel and the bootstrap host; it is not
independent signer verification.
.PP
The bootstrap-only option
.BR \-\-insecure\-skip\-checksum ,
given as the first script argument, skips the checksum step, prints a warning
to stderr, and runs the downloaded code unverified.
No hash tool is needed in that case; TLS certificate checks stay on.
The script removes the option before running
.BR sp2p ;
it is not an option of the installed command.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SP2P_SERVER
Signaling server URL (overridden by
.BR \-server ).
.TP
.B SP2P_URL
Public base URL for share links (overridden by
.BR \-url ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sp2p/config.yaml
User configuration file.
If
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is not set, defaults to
.IR ~/.config/sp2p/config.yaml .
.PP
The config file sets persistent defaults for command-line flags.
CLI flags and environment variables take precedence over config values.
.PP
Example config file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
# Default signaling server
server: https://sp2p.example.com

# Public base URL for share links
url: https://sp2p.example.com

# Default compression level (0=disabled, 1-9)
compress: 3

# Allow TURN relay without prompting
allow-relay: false

# Transport mode (auto, tcp, webrtc)
transport: auto

# Parallel TCP connections (0=auto, 1=single, 2-6=force count)
parallel: 0

# Default output directory for received files
output: ~/Downloads

# Receive and expanded archive limits (0 = 1 TiB each)
max-receive-bytes: 0
max-extract-bytes: 0

# Always show verbose output
verbose: false
.fi
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Send a single file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p send photo.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send multiple files:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p send file1.txt file2.txt file3.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send a directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p send ./my-folder
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe data from another command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ tar cf - src/ | sp2p send -name src.tar -
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receive a file using the transfer code:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p receive abc-def-ghi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Receive to a specific directory:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p receive -output ~/Downloads abc-def-ghi
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pipe received data to another command:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p receive -stdout abc-def-ghi | tar xf -
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use a self-hosted server:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p send -server https://sp2p.example.com photo.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Send with an agent-readable event stream:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p send -format json photo.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Sync a directory with rsync:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p rsync send -- -av ./photos/ sp2p::share/
$ sp2p rsync recv CODE ./backup
.fi
.RE
.PP
Forward a database port:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ sp2p tunnel serve --to tcp://127.0.0.1:5432
$ sp2p tunnel connect --listen tcp://127.0.0.1:15432 CODE
.fi
.RE
